### V23-62 (fix) — the execute endpoint now consults the request it claims to execute

The four `xfail(strict=True)` tests added above are now passing tests. `request_id` had been
reaching the handler and going nowhere but a log line; it is now the only field that matters.

`POST /api/v1/agent/coin-requests/execute` loads the row, and everything about the payment comes
from it: 404 if the hub has no such request, 409 if it is not `APPROVED`, and the amount and
destination read off the stored request rather than the POST body. A body that disagrees is
logged as a warning and ignored, which means a tampered request pays the approved recipient the
approved amount instead of being silently honoured — the mismatch shows up in the log rather
than in the treasury balance.

**Why the API key was never enough.** It answers "may this caller ask the hub to execute
something", which is a much weaker question than "may this payment happen". It is one shared
secret held by every island operator, and it was being served over plain HTTP at
`/agent/blockchain-secrets.env` (V23-58) — so the endpoint's real authority was the treasury
balance, 3.6e12, rather than any approval. Now the authority is the row, and the key only
decides who may ask.

**Replay and concurrency.** A repeat call returns 200 with the original `tx_hash` and
`already_executed: true` rather than an error. That is the case this endpoint actually meets:
the CLI posts, the hub signs and submits, the response is lost in transit. An error on retry
leaves the operator unable to tell whether it paid; the original hash lets them reconcile. Two
*simultaneous* calls are a different problem — both would pass a plain "has it executed yet"
check — so the request is claimed with a conditional
`UPDATE ... WHERE transaction_hash IS NULL` before anything is signed, and the loser of that
race gets 409. A submission that fails releases its claim, so a blockchain blip does not strand
the request; if the process dies mid-flight the row keeps a visible `claiming:<timestamp>`
marker, which fails in the direction of a stuck request rather than a double payment and can be
cleared by hand.

Success is recorded against the row (`transaction_hash` plus an audit line), which is what makes
the replay guard work at all and what makes `coin-requests list` show the payment.

Twelve tests, all passing. Six of them fail against the old handler, which is the check that
they test the fix rather than describe it. The other six are the pins that already held — the
auth gate, the balance check, and the claim-release path, which passed before only because the
old code never wrote a claim to release.

**Operational note, needs an operator decision.** The hub now refuses a `request_id` it has no
row for. Requests created against the hub's own agent WebSocket are in its database and are
unaffected. A request that only ever existed in a *follower's* database — if that flow is in use
— will now be refused, and it should be: the hub was taking the follower's word for the amount
and the destination, which is the defect. Making that flow work again needs the request
registered at the hub, not the check removed.

Separately, and not fixed here: `get_db_path()` defaults to
`/var/lib/aitbc/data/agent_coin_requests.db`, but the only such database on this host is
`hermes_coin_requests.db` (8 rows, 4 approved, 2 executed) from before the v0.5.9 rename. No
migration moved them and no deployed env file sets `AGENT_DB_PATH` or `HERMES_DB_PATH`. Reads
and writes agree with each other, so nothing is broken, but the older rows are orphaned.

---

### V23-62 (registration) — how a follower's request gets a row at the hub

The execute fix refuses a `request_id` the hub has never seen, which is correct and broke a
real flow: the hub's own database showed `req-follower-1782118019`, so requests raised on
follower islands do reach it. Those requests exist only in the island's database, so the hub
has nothing to consult. `POST /api/v1/agent/coin-requests/register` is how it gets a row.

The whole design question is what status that row may take. If registering wrote an approved
row on the caller's word, register-then-execute would hand back exactly what the execute fix
stopped giving away — an arbitrary treasury transfer for anyone holding the shared API key,
one call further along. So the hub decides, and it decides with the policy it was already
applying over the WebSocket: an agent's first request is granted automatically, anything after
that waits for a human.

That rule lived inside `request_coins_handler`, which was fine while the WebSocket was the only
way to create a request. It now lives in `services/faucet_policy.py` where both entry points
can reach it. A registered request is approved when it is the sender's first grant *and* within
`FAUCET_AUTO_APPROVE_MAX` (default 360000, the 100 AIT the hub has always granted); otherwise
it is recorded as pending with a reason the caller can read, and an operator approves it by
hand. Setting the ceiling to 0 turns automatic approval off entirely.

One deliberate difference from the WebSocket version: the prior-grant check counts approved
requests whether or not they have been executed. `agent_stream` counts only executed ones,
which is safe there because it signs immediately — the gap between approving and paying is
microseconds. Here the gap is however long the operator takes to run `execute`, so counting
only executed grants would let an agent register twice, collect two approvals and spend both.
The WebSocket copy is untouched; its narrower check is noted as debt rather than changed in a
security fix, since it means editing the live grant path.

Registration is idempotent on `request_id`, and a second registration with different terms is
refused with 409 rather than allowed to overwrite. Without that, register-100, collect the
approval, re-register-3.6e12 walks the amount up past the ceiling that just rejected it.

The CLI registers before forwarding, and stops with the hub's reason when the answer is
pending instead of forwarding an execute that would only 409.

24 tests across the two files, all passing. The ones that carry the argument are
`test_a_pending_registration_cannot_be_executed` and
`test_re_registering_with_a_different_amount_is_refused`: together they say that the two
endpoints in sequence grant no more than either grants alone, which is the property the whole
design rests on.

---

### V23-63 — the hub's genesis key was correct; the comparison was wrong

The hub could not sign anything. Every execute returned 500, and the journal said:

    GENESIS_ADDRESS (ait1fe2d63fe87db282083b9159e5857cac788af9e03) does not match the
    secp256k1 address derived from GENESIS_PRIVATE_KEY (0xFe2d63FE87Db282083b9159e5857Cac788af9E03)

Read the two strings rather than the sentence between them. `fe2d63fe87db282083b9159e5857cac788af9e03`
against `Fe2d63FE87Db282083b9159e5857Cac788af9E03` — the same forty hex characters, differing only
in the prefix and the checksum casing. It is one address written two ways, and it is the treasury
account holding 3.6e12. The key was right the whole time.

`transaction_service.py:113` compared them with `.lower()`, which normalises the casing and not
the prefix, so `ait1fe2d…` never equals `0xfe2d…` however correct the key is. `canonical_address`
was written for precisely this under V23-54, when the same mistake was stalling the block-proposer
check; `TransactionService` never picked it up. This closes the second instance.

This also revises V23-57. That finding read the same symptom as a missing or wrong treasury key on
the hub. It is neither — the key is present and correct, and no key needs to be supplied.

**Which spelling goes on the wire is a separate bug in the same lines.** The node verifies `from`
against the recovered signer canonically (`rpc/utils.verify_transaction_signature` → `canonical_address`),
so either spelling would verify. But its account lookups do not canonicalise anything — all three
of them, in `rpc/accounts.py`, `sync_state.py` and `consensus/poa.py`, match `Account.address` as an
exact string. The account is stored under whatever `GENESIS_ADDRESS` says, so signing as the derived
`0x` form asked for the nonce of an address the chain has no row for, and would have transferred
from an account that does not exist. `from` and the nonce lookup now use the configured spelling.
`get_balance` already did, which is why the balance check passed and the failure surfaced at signing.

Seven tests. Four fail against the old code, including the `ait1` case that is the hub's actual
configuration. Root 2840, blockchain-node 471, both green — this is a shared crypto library, so
the whole tree is the regression surface.

Not fixed here: the three exact-match account lookups are a latent version of the same defect, and
any account written in one spelling and queried in another will silently read as absent. That wants
a single normalisation point on the node's account access rather than three call-site patches.

---

### V23-64 — the account canonicalisation is right; it dropped escrow's foreign keys on the way

`bee3e4a83` closed the latent half of V23-63: an `AccountAddress` column type that normalises
`0x…`, `ait1…` and `aitbc1…` to one lowercase `ait1` form on both bind and result, plus the same
normalisation on trie keys. That is the right shape of fix — one normalisation point on account
access rather than patches at each call site — and it works. Two things came with it.

**The escrow foreign keys are gone.** The new columns were written as
`Field(sa_column=Column(AccountAddress(), foreign_key="account.address"))`. `foreign_key=` is
SQLModel's `Field` argument, not SQLAlchemy's `Column` argument; `Column` reads unrecognised
keywords as `<dialect>_<option>`, so it parsed this as a dialect named `foreign` with an option
`key` and dropped the constraint. SQLAlchemy said so —
`SAWarning: Can't validate argument 'foreign_key'; can't locate any SQLAlchemy dialect named 'foreign'`
— at import, where nothing was watching. `Escrow.__table__.foreign_keys` was empty. Now
`ForeignKey("account.address")`, and asserted.

**It arrived without tests, and the suite could not have caught it.** 471 tests passed both
before and after, and the SAWarning does not surface in a pytest run. Added 46, in
`test_account_address_canonicalization.py`. Most are the round trip — 25 of them are every
spelling written against every spelling queried — but two carry real weight:

- `test_the_state_root_does_not_move_for_an_already_canonical_chain`. Trie keys feed the state
  root and the state root is recorded in every block. If normalisation changed the key for
  accounts already spelled `ait1`, every historical block would fail verification and the node
  would diverge from its peers. Measured: unchanged for a canonical chain, changed for a chain
  holding any `0x`, `aitbc1` or mixed-case account. So the change is safe exactly to the degree
  the chain is already canonical, which is a fact about the deployed data, not about the code.
- `test_escrow_still_references_the_account_table`, which fails against `bee3e4a83`.

**Also folded in:** `_to_ait_address` now delegates its parsing to `canonical_address` rather
than reimplementing the prefix and hex-length rules. There are now two spellings of "canonical"
in the tree — `0x` for the signature layer, `ait1` for storage — and that is fine as long as they
agree on *what counts as an address*. Two independent copies of that rule is how they stop
agreeing, and the failure would be the chain and the signature layer disagreeing about who owns
an account.

Root 2886, blockchain-node 517, both green.

**Unresolved, and it needs the hub's data to answer.** `process_bind_param` normalises the value
in a query but does not rewrite rows already stored. Any existing account, transaction or escrow
row written as `0x…` is now unreachable — queries bind `ait1…` and miss it — and an insert for
that same account will succeed as a second row with a separate balance. The local chain database
is empty post-reset, so this could not be measured here. Before this reaches a node with history,
count the non-canonical rows and migrate them.

---

### V23-65 — the account normalisation rewrites two fields that a signature covers

The chain reset cleared the one `aitbc1` row that had already tripped this. It did not remove
the cause, and the cause is reachable from outside.

`AccountAddress` normalises on bind *and* on result. For `Account.address` that is right: it is
a lookup key, nothing signs it, and normalising it is what closed V23-63. `Transaction.sender`
and `Transaction.recipient` are a different kind of column. They are `from` and `to` in the
message the client signed, and `rpc/utils.verify_transaction_signature` rebuilds that message
out of them to check it. Rewriting either one changes the message, and a changed message does
not recover to the signer.

The node accepts a transaction signed with `0x` spellings — `verify_signature` compares the
recovered address canonically, so a hex-form client verifies fine on submission. Measured:

    verifies as submitted        : True
    submitted from / to          : 0x19E7E376E7C213B7E7e7e46cc70A5dD086DAff2A / 0xc1c1…c1
    served    from / to          : ait119e7e376e7c213b7e7e7e46cc70a5dd086daff2a / ait1c1c1…c1
    verifies as served to a peer : False

A follower replaying that block rebuilds the message from what it was served, the signature does
not recover, and `state/state_transition.py:86` returns `Invalid signature`. The follower stops
at that block. That is the shape of the stall that left it nine days and 12,287 blocks behind on
a different cause.

Nothing is broken today because every address on the post-reset chain is `ait1`, and normalising
an address already in that form is the identity. It needs one client that signs in hex form. The
API does not stop anyone being that client.

Two `xfail(strict=True)` tests state it, so CI stays green now and fails the moment it is fixed,
plus two that pass: the premise (the node really does accept a hex-form signature) and the reason
today is safe (an all-canonical transaction round-trips unchanged).

**The fix is a split, not a revert.** `Account.address` and the escrow columns keep the
normalisation — they are keys and nothing signs them. The two signature-covered columns should
store verbatim, and lookups should canonicalise the value they search *for* rather than the value
they store: given an address, search the three spellings of it. Not implemented here because
these are live consensus files under simultaneous edit from the hub, and a conflicting change to
them would cost more than a latent defect with a strict-xfail pointing at it.

---

### V23-65 (fix) — signed columns store what was signed

The two `xfail(strict=True)` tests above are now passing tests.

`Transaction.sender` and `Transaction.recipient` are plain columns again. They hold whatever
spelling the signer used, because they are `from` and `to` in the message the signature covers
and `verify_transaction_signature` rebuilds that message out of them. `Account.address` and the
escrow columns keep `AccountAddress` — they are keys, nothing signs them, and normalising them
is what closed V23-63. That split is the whole fix.

Reverting the column type was not enough on its own. Every writer was passing the canonicalised
local it had just computed for the account lookup, so the row would have been normalised anyway
with the type gone. All four now take the raw `from`/`to` off `tx_data` at the point of
construction — `consensus/poa.py` twice, `sync_block_import.py` twice — while the canonicalised
locals stay where they belong, addressing `Account` rows and keying `account_map`.

Storing verbatim would have reintroduced V23-63 for transaction history, so the three lookups
that search by address now widen instead: `lower(column) IN (ait1…, 0x…, aitbc1…)`, via
`address_spellings`. Lowercasing the column is what catches a checksummed `0x` spelling, whose
case cannot be enumerated. The mapping is one-to-one on the 40-hex body so this cannot reach a
different account, and a value that is not an address — a proposer id, an alias — is searched
for as itself rather than expanded.

18 tests in `test_signed_fields_survive_storage.py`: the round trip, the end-to-end verification
a syncing follower performs, 12 combinations of stored-spelling against searched-spelling, and
the two negative cases. blockchain-node 535, root 2904, both green.

No migration. The hub's chain was reset to an all-canonical history, so every stored row is
already `ait1`, and the widened lookup finds both those and anything written verbatim from here.

The index caveat is deliberate: `lower(column)` will not use the plain index on `sender` and
`recipient`. These three queries are balance reconciliation and transaction history, not the
block path, and a functional index can be added if either becomes hot. Correctness on money
first.

---

### V23-66 — the coin-request database claims a payout the chain has never heard of

`req-follower-1782118019` records a `transaction_hash` for a transaction that no longer exists.
The hub's chain was reset on 2026-08-15 and rebuilt from genesis; the coin-request database was
not, and nothing connects them. So it goes on asserting that a payout settled, on a chain nobody
has any more.

Fixing that one row by hand would have taken a minute and taught nothing. The two stores can
disagree by construction — one is the chain, the other is an application database beside it —
and there was no way to find out that they had.

**The chain could not be asked.** `/rpc/transactions` describes transactions in bulk with
filters for type, island and order, but there was no way to ask "do you have this hash". Added
`GET /rpc/transaction/{tx_hash}`, 404 when it does not. The 404 is the part with a consumer.

**`aitbc coin-requests reconcile`** checks every request holding a hash against the chain and
reports the ones it has never heard of. Read-only by default, so it is safe on a schedule;
`--annotate` writes the finding into each affected request's audit log and still clears nothing.

**`aitbc coin-requests reopen <id>`** clears one hash so the request can be paid again. Separate
command, one request at a time, named explicitly — because that hash is the only thing standing
between a request and a second payout, here and at `/execute`. It refuses when the chain still
holds the transaction, and refuses when the chain cannot be reached to say either way. That
second refusal is the one worth having: an unreachable node looks exactly like a missing
transaction unless the code distinguishes them, and treating them alike invites reissuing a
payout that was made perfectly well. `--force` exists and says so in the output.

For `req-follower-1782118019` specifically the answer is *not* to reopen it: `-v2` was already
issued and paid. Annotate it, so the record says what happened rather than nothing.

14 tests. The three that carry the design are the two refusals in `reopen` and
`test_annotate_records_the_discrepancy_but_still_does_not_clear_it` — annotating a whole
database must never make anything payable. blockchain-node 538, root 2918, both green.

**V23-66a — the endpoint 404'd every hash, and the test that should have caught it mocked it away.**
Reported from the hub: `reconcile` called `GET /rpc/transaction/{tx_hash}` without a `chain_id`
and got a 404 for all nine executed requests. The endpoint's fault, not the caller's —

    resolved_chain_id = get_chain_id(chain_id if chain_id else "")

`get_chain_id` falls back to the node's own chain for `None` only; an empty string comes back
unchanged, and `chain_id == ""` matches no transaction. So the documented default — omit the
parameter, get this node's chain — answered "no such transaction" for every hash on the chain.
Now `get_chain_id(chain_id or None)`.

`test_rpc_get_transaction.py` stubbed `get_chain_id` as `lambda _requested: CHAIN_ID`, which
swallows the argument and returns the right answer no matter what is passed. It pinned that the
call happened, not that it was right. It now configures `settings.chain_id` and lets the real
resolution run; the two new cases fail on the old code and pass on the new.

The hub's fix passed `chain_id` from `TransactionService().chain_id` at the call site. Reverted:
that is `CHAIN_ID` from the environment, defaulting to `ait-hub`, while the hub actually serves
`ait-hub.aitbc.bubuit.net` — a client-side guess at something the node already knows, wrong
exactly where it matters. A wrong guess 404s every hash, which reads as "not one of these
payouts happened", which is the state that invites reopening requests that were paid. The node
answers for its own chain; `--chain-id` stays available on both commands for a node serving
several islands, and is sent only when given.

blockchain-node 540, root 2922.

---

### V23-67 — one grant per agent turned out to mean one grant per *name*

`req-follower-1782118019` was granted 100 AIT to `ait1c10f…`. `req-follower-1782118019-v2` was
granted 100 AIT to the same `ait1c10f…` and auto-approved. Reported from the hub, with the
cause: the second request declared `sender="follower-ait-reset"` where the first declared
`sender="follower"`.

`has_prior_grant` keyed on `sender`. `sender` is a string in the registration body — the caller
writes it, nothing ties it to an identity, and changing it costs nothing. So the faucet's
one-grant-per-agent rule was one-grant-per-name, and `wallet_address`, the field that actually
receives the money, was never counted against at all. Rotate the name, collect again.

Now keyed on the destination as well, canonically. The canonical part is not decoration:
`ait1<body>`, `aitbc1<body>` and `0x<body>` are the same twenty bytes, so matching the stored
string exactly would have left the identical bypass to anyone who respelled the address —
V23-63 as a payout mechanism rather than a stall. The sender clause stays; it costs one `OR`
and catches an agent asking twice for two different wallets.

**The WebSocket handler had the same defect and is the worse of the two.** `/register` writes a
row an operator later executes; `agent_stream.request_coins_handler` signs and submits on the
spot, so there a bypass is a completed payout. It also required `transaction_hash IS NOT NULL`,
counting only *executed* grants — defensible while it was the only way to create a request,
since it pays microseconds after approving. `/register` is a second way in and writes approved
rows that stay unexecuted for as long as the operator takes, so the same wallet could collect
from both paths. Both now count approved rows whether or not they have been paid.

**None of the agent-coordinator tests were running.** `apps/agent-coordinator/tests` was not in
`testpaths` — not the register tests, not the `/execute` authority tests, not anything. The
suite that covers whether the hub pays out was sitting next to the code and never ran, which is
how this reached production with tests beside it that would have caught nothing anyway. Added
to `testpaths`, with `apps/agent-coordinator/src` on `pythonpath`. Root goes 2922 → 3041.

13 new tests. Seven fail on the old policy, including `test_renaming_the_sender_does_not_buy_a
_second_grant`, which is the hub's own sequence replayed.

Not fixed here, and worth an operator's attention: the two granted requests to `ait1c10f…` are
both real. The chain reset discarded the first, so only 100 AIT was ever actually paid to that
wallet — the double grant did not become a double payout, by luck rather than design.

