### V23-71b — the app tranche, and what the deletions were actually made of

49 more deleted test files across eight apps. **41 restored, 11 held back**, and the split is
the useful part: the 2026-08-14 sweep was not uniformly wrong, and it was not uniformly right.

**One restored file brought V23-69 back with it.** `apps/ai-engine/tests/test_integration_ai_engine.py`
opened with `sys.modules["numpy"] = MagicMock()` — the third copy of the assignment removed in
V23-69, still sitting in a file that had been deleted before that finding landed. It executes
at collection, so it replaced numpy for the entire run: 14 failures in
`tests/unit/test_property_crypto.py`, 8 and 6 in ai-engine's own other files. 28 of the 40
failures in the first full run came from that one line. Removed; the tests patch
`ai_service.np.*` individually, which is what they actually needed.

**Two deletions were right.** `apps/coordinator-api/tests/test_routers_agent.py` (12 tests) and
`test_routers_swarm.py` (19) exercise endpoints that only exist when `settings.debug` is true,
and both routers label them mock routes — swarm's comment reads "never enabled in production".
Making them pass means setting the flag and asserting on scaffolding. Left deleted.

**Four more were reasonable.** The pool-hub integration files wanted a `db_session` fixture
that `ea97ba9aa` removed in the same commit that deleted them. That fixture required
`POOLHUB_TEST_POSTGRES_DSN` and called `pytest.skip` without it, so those tests only ever ran
against a real PostgreSQL. They are restorable the day the test environment has one, and not
before. Worth recording that `ea97ba9aa` names every file it removed and why, and records the
commands it validated with. `ab846475c`, which took the edge and governance regression tests,
says "remove skipped, errored, and failed test functions" for 269 files.

**Six are blocked on a defect, not on their own quality.** These pass in isolation and fail in
a full run:

    apps/coordinator-api/tests/test_cross_chain_security.py
    apps/coordinator-api/tests/test_routers_users.py
    apps/coordinator-api/tests/test_v120_economic_proposals.py
    apps/coordinator-api/tests/test_quota_enforcement.py
    apps/coordinator-api/tests/test_grant_developer_smoke.py
    apps/coordinator-api/tests/test_governance_service.py
    apps/marketplace/tests/test_main.py

They fail on "no such table", and the cause is the one V23-72 describes. Every SQLModel class
in the repo registers on a single process-global `MetaData`, and three places fight over it:
`tests/integration/conftest.py` clears it outright at module scope,
`apps/blockchain-node/tests/conftest.py` removes every non-blockchain table before each of its
own tests, and governance and coordinator-api define five of the same table names. A class
registers its `Table` when its module first executes and the module then stays in
`sys.modules`, so anything removed from that registry is gone for the rest of the process --
coordinator-api's `create_all` then builds a schema with no `users` in it.

Three attempts to fix this from the test layer are worth recording as a dead end. Giving
blockchain-node's engine fixture an explicit `tables=` list, so it would stop mutating the
shared registry, took the run from 12 failures to over 300: four of its test files call
`SQLModel.metadata.create_all(engine)` unfiltered, and the destructive hook was what had been
keeping those calls narrow. The first failure was `index ix_gpu_registry_status already
exists` -- a **gpu** table, created into a blockchain test's database. Reverted. Snapshotting
coordinator-api's tables at conftest import fixed `users` and exposed `developer` and
`tenants`, which load later. Accumulating them across calls moved the failures somewhere else
again. Each attempt relocated the symptom, which is what patching around a shared mutable
global looks like. The fix belongs in V23-72.

Restored and green: 41 files across coordinator-api, blockchain-node, marketplace, trading,
pool-hub, api-gateway, blockchain-event-bridge, ai-engine, gpu and memory. Root 3552 →
**3820**, blockchain-node 540 → **640**, zero failures.

Remaining: the 101 deleted files under `tests/`, of which 22 are `tests/verification` and 9
`tests/production` — neither directory has ever been in `testpaths` either.

### V23-72 — two services, one table registry, four workarounds

Every SQLModel class in this repo registers its `Table` on one process-global `MetaData`
unless it says otherwise. `apps/governance` and `apps/coordinator-api` both model governance
and both name their tables `governance_profiles`, `proposals`, `votes`, `dao_treasury` and
`transparency_reports`. Two services, one registry, five shared names: whichever imported
second raised `Table 'governance_profiles' is already defined for this MetaData instance`, so
the two could never be imported into the same process.

Production never saw this. Each service runs alone against its own database, and the tables
are correct in both. What it broke was every attempt to run them together, and the four
workarounds it grew cost considerably more than the collision did.

| where | what it does |
|---|---|
| `tests/integration/conftest.py` | `SQLModel.metadata.clear()` at module scope |
| `apps/blockchain-node/tests/conftest.py` | removes every table it does not own, before each test |
| `apps/gpu` domain models | `__table_args__ = {"extend_existing": True}` on all 6 |
| `apps/marketplace` domain models | `__table_args__ = {"extend_existing": True}` on all 12 |

The first two are process-wide and permanent. A class registers its `Table` when its module
first executes, and the module then stays in `sys.modules`, so re-importing re-registers
nothing — a table removed that way is gone for the rest of the run. Suites collected
afterwards built schemas with tables missing from them, which is why six restored
coordinator-api files failed on "no such table: users" while passing in isolation (V23-71b).

`extend_existing` deserves its own note, because it reads like the intended answer and is not.
It does not give each service its own table. It merges the second definition into whichever
`Table` registered first, so whoever imports second silently redefines the first service's
columns, and the merge appends `Index` objects whose names are already on the table.
`create_all` then emits `CREATE INDEX ix_gpu_reviews_created_at` twice and the second fails.
The failure lands wherever `create_all` next happens to run rather than where the two
definitions met: this one surfaced in `tests/integration/test_atomic_settlement.py`, a test
that touches neither service and passes in isolation. It reads exactly like a flake.

**The fix.** A service that owns its tables declares its own `MetaData` and stops taking part:

    governance_metadata = MetaData()

    class GovernanceBase(SQLModel):
        metadata = governance_metadata

Table models subclass that instead of `SQLModel`. The duplicate definitions then coexist
without either editing the other, and `extend_existing` comes off. Both services' `storage.py`
and `alembic/env.py` point at the private registry — autogenerate in particular *must* read
the same one `create_all` writes, or it would compare the live database against an empty model
set and emit a migration dropping every table.

Done for `apps/governance` (9 models), `apps/gpu` (6) and `apps/marketplace` (12). Nothing on
disk needs migrating and no migration was run: for governance and gpu the generated DDL is
byte-identical before and after — 9 tables and 24 indexes, 6 tables and 18 indexes, same
`CREATE TABLE` and `CREATE INDEX` text in both cases.

Marketplace is the one intended difference, and it needed a decision rather than a mechanism.
Its database also received four tables it does not define, arriving on the global metadata by
transitive import from `packages/aitbc-shared`. `marketplaceoffer` is genuinely shared —
`MarketplaceOffer` is one class used by both services, with 41 references in marketplace's own
code and 4 rows in the production database — so it stays on the global registry and `init_db`
names it explicitly. `job_payments`, `marketplace_bid` and `payment_escrows` have no
references in marketplace code and 0 rows, and are no longer created. `create_all` never
drops, so the deployed database keeps the three it already has; only a fresh bootstrap differs.
Verified by running the real `init_db` against a temporary file: the diff against the previous
output is exactly those three tables and their seven indexes, and nothing else.

Root 3820 tests, blockchain-node 640, both green. The production databases under
`/var/lib/aitbc/data/` were not touched.

**What is left.** `aitbc_chain` still shares the global registry, and it is what rows 1 and 2
of that table are actually about — the coordinator's `Transaction` and the chain's collide by
name, which is what both conftest hooks exist to work around. Isolating it is the same
three-line pattern, but ~20 blockchain-node test files call
`SQLModel.metadata.create_all(engine)` unfiltered and would need to name the chain registry
instead, and it is the live blockchain package. Its own finding, not a rider on this one.

`apps/governance/tests` was expected to join `testpaths` here and does not, for a reason that
turned out to have nothing to do with metadata: adding it made five gpu and trading tests
fail. Its conftest assigns `os.environ["DATABASE_URL"]` at import — correctly, and for a
documented reason — but the assignment is process-global and never restored, and gpu, trading
and marketplace all read `DATABASE_URL` at import time. Which exposed the larger half: with
nothing setting that variable, those three suites resolve to
`/var/lib/aitbc/data/{gpu,trading,marketplace}_service.db`, the deployed databases. That is
its own finding as well, and governance joins `testpaths` when it lands.

### V23-73 — the test suite was running against the deployed databases

Adding `apps/governance/tests` to `testpaths`, which V23-72 was supposed to make possible, broke
five gpu and trading tests instead. Governance's conftest assigns `os.environ["DATABASE_URL"]`
at import — correctly, and for a reason its own docstring explains — but the assignment is
process-global and never restored, and gpu and trading both read `DATABASE_URL` at import time
to build their engines. Collecting governance moved them onto governance's database.

The interesting part is what that implies about the state before. If those suites were reading
governance's database when governance was collected, what were they reading when it was not?

    gpu_service            -> sqlite+aiosqlite:////var/lib/aitbc/data/gpu_service.db
    trading_service        -> sqlite+aiosqlite:////var/lib/aitbc/data/trading_service.db
    marketplace_service    -> sqlite+aiosqlite:////var/lib/aitbc/data/marketplace_service.db

The deployed services' databases, with the units serving them. `init_db()` runs in the FastAPI
lifespan, so `TestClient(app)` ran `create_all` against live files; `test_get_consumer_gpu_profiles`
asserted `len(profiles) > 0` and passed because production had rows, which is a test that would
fail on any clean machine and tell you nothing on this one. Governance had the same defect and
had already fixed it locally — that conftest is what the fix looked like, one service at a time.

The blockchain suite was worse, because it creates rather than reads. `/var/lib/aitbc/data/`
currently contains:

    drwxr-xr-x chain-a/     chain.db  548864 bytes
    drwxr-xr-x chain-sig/   chain.db  548864 bytes

`chain-a` and `chain-sig` are test-only chain IDs, from the `_TEST_CHAIN_IDS` set in
`apps/blockchain-node/tests/conftest.py`. Two test fixtures' databases, sitting in the deployed
data directory next to the real chain. They are inert — no service is configured for those
chain IDs — but nothing removes them, and they are left for an operator to delete rather than
deleted here.

**The fix is one variable.** `aitbc.constants.DATA_DIR` already reads `AITBC_DATA_DIR`, and
every service derives its database path from `DATA_DIR`. Setting it to a temporary directory in
the repo-root conftest moves all of them at once, including services added later — which is the
reason to fix it there rather than in a conftest per app. Three places had to stop bypassing it:
`gpu_service.storage` and `governance_service.storage` spelled `/var/lib/aitbc/...` out as a
literal default, and `aitbc_chain/config.py` kept its own `DATA_DIR = Path("/var/lib/aitbc")`,
which is the same value the shared constant resolves to except that the shared one is
overridable. Its `get_db_path` also had a "legacy path" fallback that was byte-for-byte what
the standard path already produced, and so had never once been reached; with `DATA_DIR`
following the environment it would have become a live bug, sending a temporary run back to the
deployed directory. Removed.

**A guard, because a default is not a guarantee.** A service can reach a deployed path without
going through `DATABASE_URL` at all, so `pytest_sessionfinish` walks every SQLAlchemy engine
alive at the end of the run and fails the session if any of them resolves under the deployed
data directory. It reports the URLs. That covers services nobody thought to check, which is
where all four of these came from. It is a hook rather than a test because a test would have to
import the storage modules to inspect them, and importing them is what binds the engine — the
guard would have decided the answer it was meant to measure.

**Two findings fell out of the fixtures.** `trading_service.init_db()` deliberately creates
nothing ("Schema management is Alembic's job"), so a database nobody has migrated has no tables
— which is precisely why that suite had to use the deployed file. But `alembic upgrade head`
builds `inter_chain_trades` and `island_registry` and stops: the seven tables in
`domain/trading.py` have never had a migration. **A fresh trading deployment comes up with two
of its nine tables.** The suite's fixture creates the remaining seven from the models and says
so; the migration gap itself is left as its own finding. Second, `marketplace_service.init_db`
now takes `MarketplaceOffer.__table__` off the class instead of looking the name up in
`SQLModel.metadata`, because the two conftest hooks from V23-72 unregister tables from that
registry and a name lookup then raises `KeyError` for a table that is perfectly intact.

`apps/governance/tests` joins `testpaths`, which was the point. Root 3820 → **3920**,
blockchain-node **640**, both green, and `make test-governance` is gone along with its
explanation.

### V23-74 — the chain shared its table registry with the coordinator, and two hooks paid for it

Every SQLModel class in the repo registers its `Table` on one process-global `MetaData` unless
it says otherwise. The chain is not the only thing here with a `Transaction`: `coordinator-api`
has one too, along with its own `Block` and `Receipt`. Same registry, same names, different
columns. The two never meet in production — separate processes, separate databases — so what
the collision broke was every attempt to test them in one interpreter, and the two workarounds
it grew were the most destructive things in the suite.

    tests/integration/conftest.py            SQLModel._sa_registry.dispose()
                                             SQLModel.metadata.clear()
    apps/blockchain-node/tests/conftest.py   pytest_runtest_setup -> MetaData.remove(each foreign table)

Neither is a reset. A class registers its `Table` when its module *first* executes, and the
module then stays in `sys.modules`, so re-importing re-registers nothing: a table removed that
way is gone for the rest of the process. The first ran at **module scope**, during collection,
before a single test in the repo had started — every suite collected afterwards inherited an
empty registry. The second emptied it again before each of 640 blockchain tests. That is the
whole explanation for the six coordinator-api files that failed on `no such table: users` while
passing in isolation (V23-71b), and for why V23-72's marketplace `init_db` had to take its
`Table` off the class rather than look the name up.

**The fix is the same three lines as governance, gpu and marketplace.** `aitbc_chain/metadata.py`
declares `chain_metadata` and `ChainBase`; the 21 table models across `base_models.py`,
`mempool.py` and `state/gpu_resources.py` subclass it; `database.py` and `migrations/env.py`
name that registry instead of `SQLModel.metadata`. Autogenerate has to read the same registry
`create_all` writes to, or the next revision is one that drops every table. `database.py` now
imports `MempoolEntry` and the three `gpu_resources` models explicitly, so `create_all` builds
the same 21 tables regardless of import order; the deployed chain database already has all 21,
so this changes nothing for a running node. Eighteen test files that called
`SQLModel.metadata.create_all(engine)` unfiltered now name `chain_metadata` — which is what the
blockchain conftest's hook existed to compensate for. **Both hooks are gone.**

**Removing the clear surfaced what it had been hiding.** `apps/trading` carried a fifth instance
of `extend_existing: True` — nine models colliding with coordinator-api's `contexts/trading`.
That flag does not give a service its own table; it merges the second definition into whichever
`Table` registered first, and the merge appends `Index` objects whose names are already on the
table, so `create_all` emits `CREATE INDEX ix_trade_negotiations_match_id` twice and the second
one fails. It had never failed because the metadata clear left only one definition standing.
`apps/trading` gets a `trading_metadata` of its own on the same pattern, and the flag comes off.
It comes off the chain's twenty as well: alone on its own registry, `extend_existing` can no
longer mask a cross-service collision, only a genuine duplicate definition inside the package,
which is a thing you want to hear about.

DDL was verified byte-identical before and after in both packages, by creating the schema into a
temporary SQLite file and diffing normalized `sqlite_master`:

    aitbc_chain       21 tables   88 indexes   identical
    trading_service    9 tables   31 indexes   identical

Root 3920, blockchain-node 640, both green. **V23-71c is unblocked**: the two hooks that made
those seven app test files unrestorable no longer exist.

### V23-71c — the seven files the shared registry had made unrestorable

V23-71 restored what it could and stopped at seven files that failed in a full run while
passing on their own. Six were coordinator-api files erroring on `no such table: users`, and
the cause was not in them: `apps/blockchain-node/tests/conftest.py` and
`tests/integration/conftest.py` were emptying SQLModel's global registry out from under every
suite collected after them. V23-74 removed both hooks. All seven come back, **49 tests**, root
3920 → **3969**.

Six restore unchanged. The seventh, `test_cross_chain_security.py`, was deleted for a different
reason — "missing fixtures" — and the fixture it wanted, `admin_token`, had been real: it lived
in `apps/coordinator-api/tests/conftest.py` until v0.12.0 rewrote that file wholesale for the
economics work and took it along. Ten security tests covering the cross-chain wallet trust
boundary — RPC allowlisting, private-key redaction, ownership enforcement, server-side signing —
were deleted rather than the four-line fixture put back. It is put back, next to its only
consumer.

**Two `while True` loops came out.** `test_cross_chain_security.py` and `test_routers_users.py`
both wrapped `create_all` in a loop that caught `OperationalError`, checked the message for
"already exists", and retried:

    while True:
        try:
            SQLModel.metadata.create_all(engine)
            return
        except OperationalError as exc:
            if "already exists" in str(exc):
                continue
            raise

The second file's docstring blamed "models declaring the same index both via `index=True` and
an explicit `Index(...)`". That was the wrong diagnosis of a real symptom: the duplicate
`CREATE INDEX` came from `extend_existing` merging two services' definitions of the same table
on the one global metadata, appending `Index` objects the table already had. With each service
on its own registry the loop has nothing left to catch, and both are now a plain `create_all`.

**Fourteen of the marketplace tests asserted `status_code in (200, 500)`**, on the reasoning
that the database might not be available. A test that passes on an unhandled server exception
is not testing the endpoint; it is testing that the URL routes somewhere. The database *is*
available since V23-73 — the suite builds this service's schema into a throwaway directory —
and all fourteen endpoints answer deterministically against an empty one. They now assert the
status they actually return, with the response shape where there is one worth checking. Two of
them turn out to be 404s rather than 200s.

Which surfaced an inconsistency worth recording: `GET /v1/marketplace/offers/{id}` for an offer
that does not exist returns **200 with a null body**, while its sibling
`GET /v1/marketplace/offers/{id}/history` returns 404 for the same missing offer. The route
returns `get_offer`'s `None` straight through. Asserted as it behaves, filed as its own finding.

### V23-71 tranche 3 — the 103 deleted root tests, and why `tests/` was never collected

The cleanup commits removed 103 files under `tests/`. Ten had already come back; of the
remaining 93, **90 are restored and three stay deleted**. Of the 90, **45 sit in directories
`testpaths` already names, and all 45 pass unchanged — 722 tests.** That is the whole story for
half the tranche: they were deleted as "skipped, errored, and failed" and they are none of
those.

The other 45 are the interesting half, because none of them were ever going to run wherever
they were put.

**`tests/` root is not in `testpaths`.** The list names subdirectories — `tests/unit`,
`tests/integration`, and so on — so the 58 pytest modules sitting at `tests/` itself matched
nothing. Neither did seven whole directories: `tests/agent`, `tests/api`,
`tests/contract_tests`, `tests/cross_phase`, `tests/training`, `tests/production` and
`tests/verification`. About 1,500 tests, none of which had run once.

**And `testpaths` could not simply be given `tests`, because collecting that directory hung.**
Not failed — hung, with no error and no output, forever. `tests/load_test.py` is a Locust
scenario whose name matches pytest's default `*_test.py`, so collection imports it, and
importing a Locust file pulls in gevent's monkey patching mid-collection; the process wedges on
`greenlet is being finalized`. Every subdirectory collected fine on its own, which is what made
it look like nothing was wrong. `tests/load/` already carried a `conftest.py` doing
`collect_ignore_glob = ["*.py"]` for exactly this reason, for the three scenarios in there. The
fourth, at the root, had no such cover. One `collect_ignore` later, `tests/` collects: 310
files, 4,795 tests, no errors.

`testpaths` gains `"tests"`. The individually-named entries stay — pytest deduplicates
overlapping paths, so they collect nothing extra, and each one records the finding that put it
there. Naming the directory rather than its contents is the point: a suite added later is
collected the day it lands.

**What 1,500 tests that never ran had turned into.** Four files were asserting things that had
stopped being true, and one of them mattered:

* `test_middleware_validation.py` tested a `max_response_size` constructor argument and a
  request cap read off the `Content-Length` header. v0.22's CORE-13 had deleted both ten days
  earlier and replaced them with a read of the actual body stream — *because* a chunked request
  or a spoofed `Content-Length` walked straight through the header check. The file was pinning
  the bypass rather than the guard. Rewritten against the middleware that exists, including the
  case that motivated the change: a `Content-Length: 10` on a 4KB body is refused.
* `test_validation_utils.py` called `validate_address("")` and expected a raise. That function
  was split into a non-raising `validate_address` and a raising `validate_address_strict`, and
  EIP-55 checksums are now enforced, so its "valid address" literal — mixed case, wrong
  checksum — was being asserted True of something the validator rejects. Corrected to the
  checksummed form of the same address, with the strict variant tested for the raising cases.
* `test_aitbc_logging.py` asserted that `setup_logger(structured=True)` produces a
  `StructuredFormatter`. It does not, and neither does `configure_logging(structured=True)`:
  both install `JournalFormatter` on the console unconditionally, and `structured` — along with
  `setup_logger`'s `format_string` — is read by nothing. Asserted as it behaves, with the dead
  parameter recorded as its own finding.
* `test_middleware.py` asserted that the performance and request-ID middlewares log at `info`.
  The levels were since split so the routine case is `debug` and only slow or failing requests
  reach `warning`, which is deliberate and is why the journal is readable.

**Three files stay deleted, and that one was a correct deletion.**
`tests/test_coordinator_api.py`, `test_coordinator_api_extended.py` and
`test_coordinator_api_v1.py` hold 135 tests, of which 121 assert some form of
`status_code in (...)`:

    94  status_code in (200, 404, 500)
     7  status_code in (200, 403, 404, 500)
     6  status_code in (200, 401, 403, 404, 500)
     …
     1  status_code in (400, 404, 422, 500)

A test that accepts 200 and 404 and 500 from the same call passes whether the endpoint works,
does not exist, or crashes. They also now fail — the routes answer 401 since auth was
hardened — and the only available repair is to widen the tuples further. coordinator-api has a
real suite of its own at `apps/coordinator-api/tests`, collected since V23-69. These add
nothing to it.

**The `tests/verification` gate named three modules and seven needed gating.** That directory's
`conftest.py` skips production-probing scripts unless `AITBC_ALLOW_PRODUCTION_WRITE_TESTS=1`,
because several of its files are not tests: they read the live mainnet head from
`https://hub.aitbc.bubuit.net/rpc` and POST newly constructed blocks back to it, and one of
them creates jobs, polls as a miner, submits results and triggers a refund against the live
coordinator. A passing run means that happened. The gate listed `test_block_import`,
`test_block_import_complete` and `test_cross_node_blockchain`. It missed `test_minimal`,
`test_simple_import`, `test_tx_import` — each POSTing to `/importBlock` — and
`test_payment_integration`. A hand-maintained list of which files touch production will be
wrong again the next time one is added, so the gate now reads the file instead: any module
whose source names a deployment host is skipped, matched against source text rather than by
importing, so nothing in a gated module executes. Deliberately blunt, and blunt in the safe
direction.

One file in that directory is not Python at all: `verify_gpu_deployment.sh`, an operator
script that ssh's to the container and reports on the two GPU units. Restoring it tripped
V23-23's convert-on-touch rule, so it gains `set -euo pipefail` — and the two
`systemctl status` calls gain `|| true`, because that command exits non-zero for a stopped
unit and a stopped unit is the most useful thing the script can report. Without the guard,
`set -e` would abort before the port and registration checks, leaving a diagnostic that only
completes when there is nothing to diagnose.

**Three things the new tests caught immediately**, all of them ours, and all in code no suite
had been able to exercise:

* Three test harnesses — `tests/fixtures/multi_chain.py`, `tests/harness/multi_node.py`,
  `tests/harness/settlement_chaos.py` — build chain databases with
  `SQLModel.metadata.create_all`. V23-74 moved the chain models to `chain_metadata`, so that
  call had started creating nothing and every use failed with `no such table: account`. Nothing
  had said so, because the file that exercises them, `tests/test_multi_chain_fixtures.py`, is
  one of the 58 at `tests/` root. Fixed in V23-74.
* V23-73's own guard crashed. It walks `gc.get_objects()` for engines bound to deployed
  databases, and that walk sees unfinished objects: an `AsyncEngine` with no `sync_engine` yet
  raises from `.url` rather than answering. With ~1,500 more tests in the run, one was in that
  state at teardown, and the session exited 1 with zero failures reported and a pluggy
  traceback where the summary should be. An engine that cannot say where it points cannot be
  checked, so it is skipped. Fixed in V23-73.
* **The V23-73 defect itself, recurring in a restored file.** With the tranche collected, 58
  tests failed across four `apps/trading` files — all with
  `The asyncio extension requires an async driver to be used. The loaded 'pysqlite' is not
  async.` Not one of the four fails on its own. `tests/integration/test_coordinator_api.py` has
  three fixtures that did `os.environ["DATABASE_URL"] = f"sqlite:///{db_path}"` inside a
  `TemporaryDirectory`, and nothing undid the assignment: the variable outlived the fixture,
  the temp file it named outlived nothing, and every suite collected afterwards inherited a
  URL pointing at a deleted path with a sync driver spelled into it. `trading_service.storage`
  reads `DATABASE_URL` at import to build an **async** engine, so it got pysqlite and said so
  1,400 tests later. Now `monkeypatch.setenv`, which pytest restores at teardown, and the URL
  names `sqlite+aiosqlite`. This is the exact shape of what V23-73 fixed in
  `apps/governance/tests/conftest.py` — process-global `DATABASE_URL` assignment with no
  restore — and it was invisible until enough of the suite ran in one process for a second
  service to read the variable.

Root **3,969 → 6,361**, green; blockchain-node **640**, green. `tests/production` reports its
own skips: those suites want an agent coordinator on localhost:9001 and say so at the end of the
run rather than passing quietly, which is what its conftest was written for.

### V23-75 — `alembic upgrade head` built two of trading's nine tables · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

`apps/trading` manages its schema with Alembic and means it: `storage.init_db()` creates
nothing, with a comment saying so — *"Schema management is Alembic's job. Do not call
create_all() here. Run `alembic upgrade head` before service startup."* That is the right
policy. It just was not true that the migrations built the schema.

They built `inter_chain_trades` and `island_registry`. The seven tables in
`domain/trading.py` — `trade_requests`, `trade_matches`, `trade_negotiations`,
`trade_agreements`, `trade_settlements`, `trade_feedback`, `trading_analytics` — had never had
a migration in the service's life. **A fresh deployment of this service came up with two of
its nine tables**, and every route touching a trade would have answered `no such table`.

The deployed database is not missing them, which is why nobody noticed: it holds all nine
because something ran `create_all` against it before that comment was written. The same
accident left four of *coordinator-api's* tables in it — `job_payments`, `payment_escrows`,
`marketplaceoffer`, `marketplace_bid` — sitting in the trading service's file, which is the
V23-72/73/74 shared-registry defect visible on disk. Trading imports none of them. They are
left alone here; removing tables from a deployed database is an operator decision, and they
are empty.

Migration 002 already names columns in four of the seven missing tables. Its guard skips
tables that do not exist, so on a fresh database it ran and migrated nothing, silently. A
migration that quietly succeeds against a table that was never created is the shape of this
whole finding.

**Reconciling head against the models turned up three more gaps in the two tables 001 did
build.** The fix is scoped to the invariant rather than the symptom: after 003,
`alembic upgrade head` reproduces `trading_metadata` exactly.

* **Five columns.** `inter_chain_trades` never got the v0.9.0 HTLC fields — `escrow_id`,
  `settlement_phase`, `secret_hash`, `source_timelock`, `dest_timelock`. The models have had
  them since v0.9.0; no migration added them. Added with server defaults matching the models'
  Python-side defaults, so the `NOT NULL` is satisfiable on a table with rows.
* **Twenty-seven indexes.** Twenty-one belong to the seven new tables. The other six the
  models declare on the two old ones, and 001 never created: five on `inter_chain_trades`,
  one on `island_registry`.
* **Two duplicate indexes.** 001 creates `idx_inter_chain_trades_source_chain` and
  `idx_inter_chain_trades_dest_chain`, which the models do not declare and which duplicate
  `ix_inter_chain_trades_source_chain` and `ix_inter_chain_trades_dest_chain` column for
  column. The deployed database has both pairs. The models are what autogenerate compares
  against, so the duplicates are dropped rather than back-filled into the models.

**And the money columns.** 002 converts the ten `Float` money columns to `Numeric(20, 8)` on
PostgreSQL and returns early on SQLite, reasoning that the SQLite type is advisory. That is
true of the stored value and worth stating precisely, because it is easy to read as a bug:
SQLite gives *both* `FLOAT` and `NUMERIC` declarations REAL affinity, so
`Decimal("12345678901.12345678")` comes back rounded either way — the conversion buys no
precision, and 002 was not losing any by skipping it. It is not true of the schema. Alembic
compares column types by default, so leaving them `FLOAT` means every future
`--autogenerate` re-reports the same ten changes forever, and a real change hides in the
noise. 003 converts whichever are still `Float`, through batch mode, which is the supported
path on SQLite.

One trap worth recording, because it silently produced a no-op that looked like success:
`sa.Float` **subclasses** `sa.Numeric`. The obvious guard — skip the column if its reflected
type is already `Numeric` — skips every column including the ones that need converting. The
condition that means "not converted yet" is `isinstance(type_, sa.Float)`.

**Verification.** The schema is compared object by object against `trading_metadata`, with
server defaults normalized out — the migrations declare them and the models express them in
Python, which 001 established and 003 follows.

    fresh database          40 objects   matches the models
    upgraded deployed copy  40 objects   matches the models

Both, not just the first: the deployed database sat at revision **001** — 002 had never been
applied to it — and it already held all nine tables, so every operation in 003 is guarded and
the run against a *copy* of the real file was the test of those guards. `alembic upgrade head`
→ `downgrade base` → `upgrade head` reproduces an identical schema, and `--autogenerate`
against head now reports no changes at all.

**The test stopgap is gone, and its absence is the test.** `apps/trading/tests/conftest.py`
ran `alembic upgrade head` and then `trading_metadata.create_all` on top, because the
migrations covered two tables — a stopgap added in V23-73 and recorded as a finding at the
time. It now runs the migrations alone. If a model is added without a migration, the suite
fails with `no such table` instead of papering over exactly the gap a deployment would hit.
58 tests, green.

**What this does not fix: nothing runs the migrations.** `init_db()` says to run
`alembic upgrade head` before service startup, and no systemd unit in the repo has an
`ExecStartPre` that does — not trading's, not any of the twenty. The deployed trading database
is still at 001. Filed separately; it needs an operator, and it is not trading-specific.

### V23-76 — `GET /offers/{id}` told clients a missing offer existed · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

`GET /v1/marketplace/offers/{offer_id}` answered **200 with a body of `null`** for an offer
that is not there. The route called `MarketplaceService.get_offer`, which returns `None` when
nothing matches, and returned it. FastAPI serialized `None` to `null` and sent it with the
default status.

A client that checks the status code — which is the normal thing to do, and what every HTTP
library's `raise_for_status` does — was told the offer existed and then had to discover
otherwise by finding `None` where a field should be. The failure surfaces one layer away from
its cause, in whatever tried to read `.price_per_hour` off nothing.

**It was the only route in the service that did this.** `get_offer` has four callers in
`main.py`; the other three — `/offers/{id}/history`, `/offers/{id}/cancel` and
`/dynamic-pricing` — all check and return
`JSONResponse(status_code=404, content={"error": "Offer not found"})`. So do
`/offer/{plugin_id}` and `/offer-by-id/{offer_id}` for their own lookups. Seven "not found"
paths in one file, six of them agreeing, and the seventh over the same resource as one of the
six: `/offers/{id}` said 200-null while `/offers/{id}/history` said 404 for the identical
missing offer.

Fixed to match its siblings exactly — same status, same body — rather than by picking the
form FastAPI would idiomatically use. `HTTPException` would have produced `{"detail": ...}`,
which is the one shape *nothing* in this service returns; there is a single `HTTPException` in
the file against twelve `JSONResponse`s. Moving one route from disagreeing about the status
code to disagreeing about the body shape would not have been a fix.

The test that pinned this is updated rather than deleted. It came out of V23-71c, where
fourteen marketplace assertions of the form `status_code in (200, 500)` were tightened to the
codes the endpoints actually return; two turned out to be 404s, and this one turned out to be
the 200-null. It was asserted as it behaved, with a docstring recording why, and the
inconsistency filed. It now asserts 404 **and the body**, because the finding was that this
route disagreed with the others — a 404 in some other shape would only have relocated the
disagreement.

One thing this does not fix, noticed while checking whether any client depended on the old
behaviour: `docs/api/marketplace-openapi.json` documents `200` and `422` for all eight
`/offers/{...}` and `/offer/{...}` operations, including the six that have always returned
404. The spec is generated by `scripts/extract_openapi_specs.py` from FastAPI's own schema
inference, which cannot see a `JSONResponse` constructed inside a handler — so no 404 in this
service has ever appeared in its published contract. That is why the inconsistency was
invisible from the documentation: the docs said all eight behaved the same way, and they were
wrong about six of them rather than right about any. Filed separately; documenting one route's
404 while five stay undocumented would make the spec less consistent, not more.

No consumer relied on the old form. The endpoint's callers are `tests/load/locustfile.py`,
which reads an ID out of a live listing and already treats any non-200 as a failure, and the
agent SDK, which only `PUT`s. `apps/exchange` matches the same path prefix in its own handler
and does not call this service.

Marketplace suite green; root **6,361** collected, exit 0.

### V23-77 — node startup could not tell a corrupt schema from a second start · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

`init_db` wrapped `chain_metadata.create_all(engine)` in an `except Exception` that re-raised
only when the message did **not** contain "already exists". The reported finding is that this
swallows real errors. Verifying it turned up three more defects in the same twelve lines, all
in the path every node runs on every start, and none of them covered by a test — the two
functions involved had no test file at all before this.

**1. The handler was dead, and could only ever have been harmful.**

`create_all` defaults to `checkfirst=True`: it queries the existing schema and emits DDL only
for what is missing, so "already exists" is not an error it produces. What it *did* raise, until
V23-74, was a duplicate `CREATE INDEX` — every service shared one global SQLModel `MetaData` and
`extend_existing` appended index objects a table already had. That is fixed at the source;
`aitbc_chain` owns its tables now.

Verified rather than reasoned: the startup path was run twice each against copies of all eight
chain databases on this host — the live hub (21 tables, 4,212 blocks), `ait-hub` (15 tables),
the legacy 5-table `chain.db`, `chain-a`, and the partial and brand-new fixtures. No raise, in
sixteen runs. Every one of those ran against a **copy** in a scratch directory; nothing under
`/var/lib/aitbc/` was opened for writing.

Matching on an exception's *message* was never a safe way to sort benign collisions from
corruption in any case — that wording belongs to SQLAlchemy and pysqlite, not to us, and it
moves between versions. A disk I/O error, a malformed file, a permissions failure: all
re-raised, correctly, but only because the string happened not to match. The handler is gone.

**2. `ALTER TABLE transaction ADD COLUMN ...` is a syntax error.**

`_migrate_existing_columns` — which runs immediately after, and adds columns to tables that
already exist — interpolated table and column names into DDL bare. `transaction` is a SQLite
keyword, so the statement it builds for that table does not parse:

    sqlite3.OperationalError: near "transaction": syntax error

Reproduced against a copy of the deployed `/var/lib/aitbc/data/chain.db` with one column staged
missing, which is the state a node upgrade leaves behind. It is exactly **one** identifier out
of the 164 in this schema that needs quoting, which is why nothing has tripped over it — and it
names the chain's central table, the one most likely to gain a column. The failure mode is not a
skipped migration; it is an unhandled exception in `init_db`, so the node does not start.

Identifiers now go through `engine.dialect.identifier_preparer.quote`. The existing
`_is_valid_sql_identifier` check stays: it rejects a name outright, where quoting only makes one
safe to interpolate, and it is worth keeping the note that it returns `True` for `transaction`.

**3. Callable defaults were interpolated by `repr`.**

The same function built its DEFAULT clause with an f-string over `col.default.arg`. For a
`Field(default_factory=lambda: datetime.now(UTC))` that attribute is the function object:

    ALTER TABLE account ADD COLUMN updated_at DATETIME DEFAULT <function Account.<lambda> at 0x7f...>

24 columns in this schema have a callable default — every `created_at`, `updated_at` and
`timestamp` on every core table — and all 24 are NOT NULL, so none of them could have been
skipped. The factory is now evaluated once and the result rendered by the dialect
(`DEFAULT '2026-08-16 10:14:22.936346'`), which is what a hand-written backfill does. Literals go
through the same path instead of `isinstance(val, str)` and an f-string, so the dialect owns the
quoting for every type rather than for the one that was special-cased.

**4. Everything else got `DEFAULT ''`.**

The remaining branch read `elif not col.nullable: default = " DEFAULT ''"`, under a comment about
text columns. **91 columns** in this schema are NOT NULL with no model-side default, and only 68
of them are textual. The branch was writing:

    block.height                     INTEGER  DEFAULT ''
    escrow.amount                    INTEGER  DEFAULT ''
    transaction.payload              JSON     DEFAULT ''
    governance_proposal.voting_ends  DATETIME DEFAULT ''
    mempool.received_at              FLOAT    DEFAULT ''

None of that raises. SQLite accepts the DDL and stores the empty string on the first insert that
omits the column — a block at height `''`, an escrow holding `''` tokens. This is the worst of
the four precisely because it is the one that appears to work.

A NOT NULL column with no default genuinely cannot be back-filled into a table that has rows;
there is no value to give the rows already there, and SQLite refuses it too. So the function now
raises, naming the column and pointing at `migrations/`, instead of inventing a value that parses
into the wrong one. It is unreachable for any table `create_all` built — that builds the column —
which is why all eight database copies migrate clean; it fires only for a table that predates a
column the models now require, and that is a case for a real migration written by a human.

**Tests.** `apps/blockchain-node/tests/test_schema_bootstrap.py`, five of them, against the two
functions that had none. Bootstrapping three times over the same database pins that no exception
handling is needed. A column dropped from `transaction` and re-added pins the quoting, and would
have failed with the syntax error above. The broad one drops all 103 back-fillable columns across
20 of the 21 tables and asserts every one comes back — the state the hub's database was in, at
the width the function claims to cover. `escrow.amount` dropped and refused by name pins the
raise. The last asserts that `transaction` is still the *only* identifier of the 164 that needs
quoting, so a future `order` or `group` column is named by a test rather than by a node that will
not start.

Blockchain-node **645** collected (640 + 5), 12 skipped; root **6,366**, exit 0.

### V23-78 — a log format switch that has not been read since June · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

`setup_logger` and `configure_logging` both took a `structured: bool` they never read;
`setup_logger` also took a `format_string` it never read. That is the reported finding, and it
is only the near end of it. The same switch had a third face — a `LOG_FORMAT` environment
variable, read by `_get_log_format()`, which had no callers — and *that* one was wired into
production: `LOG_FORMAT=json` was set in **five systemd units** and asserted by **two release
gates**, one of which fails the release if it is missing.

**It was disconnected on purpose, and the cleanup was never done.** `1b81d840` (19 Jun 2026,
"switch systemd journal logging to compact human-readable format") replaced the conditional
that chose between formatters:

```python
-        if structured or _get_log_format() == "json":
-            include_timestamp = to_file
-            formatter: logging.Formatter = StructuredFormatter(include_timestamp=include_timestamp)
-        else:
-            if format_string is None:
-                format_string = "[%(levelname)s] %(message)s"
-            formatter = logging.Formatter(format_string)
-        console_handler.setFormatter(formatter)
+        console_handler.setFormatter(JournalFormatter())
```

Its reasoning was sound and is not disturbed here: JSON in the systemd journal is unreadable,
journald keeps its own structure, and file output — which is what aggregators consume — was
already `StructuredFormatter` unconditionally. Console text, file JSON. The defect is that the
commit removed the conditional and left every way of asking for the branch it deleted.

So this finishes that commit rather than reversing it. Restoring the switch would have been the
other option and is the wrong one twice over: it would revert a deliberate decision, and it
would do so by flipping five deployed services to JSON journal output at their next restart.

Removed: `structured` and `format_string` from `setup_logger`, `structured` from
`configure_logging`, `_get_log_format()`, and its re-export from `aitbc/log_utils/logging.py`.

Removed with them, because leaving them would leave the tree asserting something false:

* `Environment=LOG_FORMAT=json` from `aitbc-blockchain-node.service`,
  `aitbc-blockchain-p2p.service`, `aitbc-coordinator-api.service`, `aitbc-marketplace.service`
  and `aitbc-pool-hub.service` — along with the comment above four of them explaining that it
  is placed after `EnvironmentFile=` so secrets cannot clobber it. Someone debugged that
  ordering problem for a setting that does nothing.
* `check_json_logging()` from `scripts/check-production-readiness.py`, and step 6 of
  `scripts/release/check-v0.5.0.sh`, which exits 1 when coordinator-api lacks the variable.
  Both would have started failing the moment the units were cleaned, which is a fair summary
  of what they were worth: they verified that an operator had set a variable, never that any
  logging happened.
* The two `LOG_FORMAT` lines in `docs/deployment/STAGING.md`.

**No caller passed either parameter.** `setup_logger` has no production callers at all — every
call in the tree is in tests — and all 40-odd `configure_logging` sites pass keywords only, so
removing a parameter from the middle of the signature shifts nothing. That is worth stating
explicitly: `structured` sat between `level` and `service_name`, and a single positional caller
would have made this a silent behaviour change instead of a deletion.

Left alone deliberately: `docs/releases/v0.4.25/logging-patterns.md`, `v0.5.0/change.log` and
`v0.5.1/change.log` all describe `LOG_FORMAT` as live. They are versioned records of releases
that shipped before it was disconnected, and they were accurate when written. Rewriting them
would be rewriting history — but note that `logging-patterns.md` reads as guidance rather than
as a change record, so anyone who finds it will be misinformed. There is no unversioned logging
doc for it to be corrected into; that is its own gap.

The tests are the other half. Seven call sites passed one of the dead parameters, and four of
them then attached a handler of their own — three carrying a `StructuredFormatter`, one a plain
`logging.Formatter` — so every assertion in those four ran through a formatter the test itself
installed. They would have passed identically with the argument deleted, which is exactly what
has now happened to them. The fourth, `test_traditional_format_string`, differed from the test
directly above it by nothing but the dead kwarg, and is deleted rather than kept.

Replacing the three that asserted against the parameters are two that state the contract the
refactor actually chose: console is always `JournalFormatter`, and a file handler, when one is
configured, is always `StructuredFormatter`. Both assert the full handler list rather than
`handlers[0]`, so a second handler appearing is a failure rather than something skipped over.

Nothing was restarted and no unit was reloaded. The five unit files are symlinked into this
working tree from `/etc/systemd/system`, so the edits are live on disk, but removing a variable
no code reads cannot change a running service, and the next ordinary restart picks it up.

Root **6,365**, exit 0.

### V23-79 — a first install never migrated, and the check could not say so · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

Filed out of V23-75 as *"nothing runs the migrations… no systemd unit in the repo has an
`ExecStartPre` that does"*. **The first half of that was wrong and I wrote it.**
`update.sh` step 5 has run `alembic upgrade head` for every service with an `alembic.ini`
since 1 July 2026, and does it carefully — per-service env file, service stopped for the
duration, blockchain-node deliberately skipped. What is true is narrower and worse:

* **`deploy.sh` never migrated at all.** `main` runs `initialize_databases` — which creates
  Postgres databases and nothing else — then `setup_systemd_services`, then
  `start_services`. So the *install* path brings every service up against whatever schema
  happens to exist, which for trading, whose `init_db()` creates nothing on purpose, is
  none. Only the *update* path migrated, and that one presupposes an installed node.
* **The check that should have caught it cannot fail for the cases that are real.**

Both are fixed here. The `ExecStartPre` the original note asked for is not, deliberately —
see the last section.

**`scripts/deployment/run-migrations.sh`** is now the single implementation, exec'd by
`update.sh` as its step 5 and by `deploy.sh` after the units are linked and before the
services start. That ordering is load-bearing in both directions: the script skips any
service whose unit is absent, which is how it stays role-aware, and migrating a running
service is the thing it stops to avoid.

#### The readiness check had three defects, and they compounded

`check_migrations()` in `scripts/check-production-readiness.py` discovers `apps/*/alembic.ini`
and runs `alembic current` in each. It was not run whole against this host, on purpose — doing
so connects to production databases with production credentials, which is exactly what a
verification step must not do casually. The three defects below were each established
separately, from the code and from read-only reads, and each is now pinned by a test.

**A database that has never been migrated passed.** `alembic current` against a database
with no `alembic_version` table exits 0 and writes nothing to stdout — the banner and the
INFO lines go to stderr. That was read as `no revision applied (skipped)` and, unlike every
other branch, left `all_ok` untouched. So the worst state a database can be in was the one
state treated as acceptable, while being a single revision behind was a failure. Confirmed
on a copy of the deployed gpu database:

```
$ alembic current            # scratchpad copy of /var/lib/aitbc/data/gpu_service.db
alembic: target database -> sqlite:////…/gpu_copy.db          <- stderr
INFO  [alembic.runtime.migration] Context impl SQLiteImpl.    <- stderr
INFO  [alembic.runtime.migration] Will assume non-transactional DDL.
exit=0, stdout 0 bytes
```

Every line an operator would read as evidence goes to stderr; the stream the check parses is
empty, and empty is indistinguishable from "nothing to say".

**blockchain-node was excluded from the check entirely.** `_has_migrations` built
`app_dir / script_location / "versions"` from the raw ini value. blockchain-node is the only
app that writes `script_location = %(here)s/migrations` — Alembic's own token for the ini
file's directory — so the check looked for `apps/blockchain-node/%(here)s/migrations/versions`,
found no directory, and dropped the app without a word in its output — seven revisions, and
the one app where the default target is provably not the database the nodes use (V23-49),
silently outside the check's scope.

**For coordinator-api it read the wrong database.** It ran `alembic current` with no service
environment, so each `env.py` fell back to its own default target. coordinator-api's
`env.py` takes `DATABASE_URL or SQLITE_URL or app_settings.database.effective_url`, and with
none of the first two set that resolves to `sqlite:////var/lib/aitbc/data/coordinator.db`
while `/etc/aitbc/aitbc-coordinator-api.env` puts the service on Postgres. So the check
reported confidently on a SQLite file the service does not open. This is the same bug
`271d395f2` fixed in `update.sh` in August — the fix went to the migrator and not to the
checker, which is how the two came to disagree about which database a service has. They now
read the same two filenames in the same order.

pool-hub escapes this one by luck rather than design: `migrations/env.py` and
`settings.py` each hardcode the same `postgresql+asyncpg://poolhub:poolhub@127.0.0.1:5432/aitbc`,
and `/etc/aitbc/aitbc-pool-hub.env` sets no `DATABASE_URL` at all — it carries one variable,
a shared secret. Two copies of a DSN that agree today are not a service environment, and the
next edit to either is what turns pool-hub into the coordinator-api case.

#### What the deployed databases actually look like

Read-only, from the SQLite files, the pool-hub Postgres database, and each app's revision
graph. This is the state the check was reporting no problem about:

| service | deployed | head | |
|---|---|---|---|
| gpu | *no `alembic_version`* | `b8f3a2c91d04` | never migrated |
| edge | *no `alembic_version`* | `e1c47b09f2a6` | never migrated |
| pool-hub | *no `alembic_version`* | `e5d4f8a9b0c1` | never migrated |
| trading | `001` | `003` | behind |
| governance | `002` | `003` | behind |
| blockchain-node | `d4e8b91c0a37` | `d4e8b91c0a37` | at head, on the file no node uses |
| coordinator-api | **not read** | `a3e7c15b8d94` | see below |

gpu and edge gained Alembic in V23-47 and have not been migrated since. pool-hub's is the
`aitbc` Postgres database at the DSN both its `env.py` and its settings hardcode; there is no
`alembic_version` relation in it. The blockchain-node row is the V23-49 decoy — the default
target, `/var/lib/aitbc/data/chain.db`, is at head while the live island databases are
elsewhere; four of its seven revisions branch and remerge, so `d4e8b91c0a37` is the single
head only by way of the `459d59e234e4` merge.

**coordinator-api's live revision is unknown and this entry does not guess at it.** Reading
it means connecting to Postgres with the credentials in `/etc/aitbc/aitbc-coordinator-api.env`,
which I am not permitted to do and did not attempt. What is measured is the SQLite file the
unpatched check was reading instead — `/var/lib/aitbc/data/coordinator.db`, at
`e9cf23ae4640` against a head of `a3e7c15b8d94`, 32 revisions in the graph. That file being
behind says nothing about the service; it is only evidence that the check was answering
about the wrong database. The operator has to read the real one.

Five of the seven are known to need an operator and the sixth is unknown; **none of it was
applied here**, and the commands are at the end.

#### Why not `ExecStartPre`

The original note asked for one and this does not add it, because on this system it is worse
than what it replaces:

* Four blockchain-node units — `node`, `p2p`, `rpc`, `sync` — run as the same user against
  the same SQLite chain database. An `ExecStartPre` on each is four concurrent
  `upgrade head` on one file.
* blockchain-node's Alembic default is a database no node uses (V23-49), so the unit would
  have to know its own island, which is exactly what `run-migrations.sh` refuses to guess.
* These migrations go through `batch_alter_table(recreate="always")`, which drops and
  rebuilds the table. `run-migrations.sh` stops the service first. A unit cannot do that for
  its own siblings, and a unit that stopped the service it is starting is a cycle.
* A failed migration in `ExecStartPre` turns one bad revision into a restart loop across
  every service that shares the database.

Migrations are a deploy step, taken with the services stopped. A test asserts no unit
invokes `alembic`, so a later sweep does not add one by reflex.

#### Incidental

* `.gitignore` ignores `scripts/deployment/*` behind a six-entry allowlist, so
  `run-migrations.sh` was untracked on creation and would have been committed by nobody.
  Added to the allowlist rather than force-added. The rule only ever bites new files — 33 of
  the 35 files in that directory were already tracked, and with this one that becomes 34.
  The remaining one is `deploy_agent_dao.py`, present on disk and ignored by the same rule;
  whether it should be tracked is somebody's decision and not this PR's. Worth knowing before
  adding the next script there: `git status` will not tell you it was skipped.
* `update.sh` gained `set -o pipefail`. Its only one lived in the migration subshell that
  moved out, and this replaces it rather than losing it. Not a behaviour change: without
  `set -e` a pipeline's status matters only where tested, and of the four tested, two are
  `systemctl … | grep -q` where a failing `systemctl` produces no output and the `grep`
  fails anyway, and two are `||` between commands rather than pipelines.
* `deploy.sh` is added to `check-shell-strict-mode.py`'s `SKIP_SETTINGS` for `-u` and
  `pipefail`, with the reason recorded there. It has `set -e`, and
  `LATEST_BACKUP=$(ls -t "$BACKUP_DIR"/backup_* 2>/dev/null | head -1)` fails its `ls` on a
  host with no backups while `head` succeeds — under `pipefail` the assignment aborts the
  script, turning "no backup found", which the next line handles, into a silent exit on the
  rollback path. That is the V23-23 hazard arriving through the check meant to prevent it.
* `check-production-readiness.py`'s docstring still advertised "Log format is JSON" after
  V23-78 removed that check. Mine to fix, fixed here.

#### Verification

`run-migrations.sh` was exercised against a stub host — `systemctl` and `alembic` replaced
on `PATH`, `AITBC_ROOT` pointed at a scratch tree — covering the migrate path (correct cwd,
PYTHONPATH and env file), the blockchain-node skip with its per-island instructions, a
failing migration (`0 ok, 1 failed`, exit 1, services restarted), and a missing venv. **No
service was stopped, started or reloaded, and no database was written.** The survey above
opened the deployed SQLite files through `file:…?mode=ro` URIs and pool-hub's Postgres with a
single `SELECT`; the only database written to at any point was a scratchpad copy of
`gpu_service.db`.

The `check_migrations` tests run against a temporary apps tree and a stub `alembic` rather
than the real one, because this check's job is to connect to production databases with
production credentials and a test suite must not.

Root **6,384**, exit 0.

#### Operator — bringing the deployed databases to head

None of this was applied. Migrations run best through the deploy path, which stops each
service first:

```
sudo /opt/aitbc/scripts/deployment/run-migrations.sh
```

That covers gpu, edge, pool-hub, trading, governance and coordinator-api, each under its own
`/etc/aitbc` env file. Three cautions:

* **governance is at `002` and 003 is `003_v023_voting_ends_block`.** Do not migrate
  `/var/lib/aitbc/data/governance_service.db` without taking a copy first and reading V23-73
  — 002 was rewritten into raw SQL and the file is live.
* **coordinator-api's live revision has not been read.** Before running anything, read it:
  `alembic current` from `apps/coordinator-api` with the service env loaded, and confirm the
  URL it prints is the Postgres one and not `/var/lib/aitbc/data/coordinator.db`. It is 32
  revisions and the largest schema here; how far behind it is decides whether this is a
  no-op or a maintenance window.
* **blockchain-node is skipped by design.** It names each island for you; run those
  explicitly, with the node stopped, and check the target path before pressing return.

Afterwards, `scripts/check-production-readiness.py` answers the question — which, before
this change, it could not.

### V23-80 — 703 operations, no 404s, and a drift guard nobody had run · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

Filed out of V23-76, which fixed `GET /v1/marketplace/offers/{id}` answering 200-null for a
missing offer and noted that the published spec documented `200` and `422` for all eight
`/offers/{...}` operations — including the six that had always returned 404. Measured across
all five published specs, it is not six operations:

| | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| operations | 698 | 700 |
| documenting a 404 | **0** | 119 |
| documenting any 4xx/5xx other than 422 | **0** | 804 across 507 operations |

Not one 401, 403, 404, 409 or 5xx appeared anywhere in `docs/api/`. FastAPI infers responses
from signatures — the return annotation gives 200, a request model gives 422 — and a
`raise HTTPException(404, …)` in a function body appears in neither. So the mechanism every
service in this repository uses to say "not found" was, by construction, the one thing its
contract could not express.

That is why V23-76 was invisible from the documentation. The spec said all eight offer
operations behaved identically; they did not, and the spec was wrong about six of them rather
than right about any. A client generated from that file has no branch for a 404 at all.

#### Derived, not annotated

`scripts/openapi_error_responses.py` reads each handler's source and reports what it
constructs: the status code from `HTTPException`, `JSONResponse`, `Response` and
`PlainTextResponse`, the body shape, and the message where it is a literal.
`extract_openapi_specs.py` applies it as a pass over the generated document.

Hand-annotating `responses={...}` on 703 operations was the alternative and it is worse in
the way that matters: it would be correct at the moment of writing and unfalsifiable
afterwards. A derived spec is checkable — `make openapi-check` regenerates and diffs, so a
handler and its documentation cannot drift apart silently.

Three decisions inside the scan are worth stating because each one could have gone the
plausible-but-wrong way:

* **Only literals, and only from the handler's own source and its route dependencies.** A
  status code computed at runtime, or raised inside a service method the handler calls, is
  not documented. This makes the output a **floor** — everything documented is genuinely
  reachable, and some reachable responses are still missing. Both directions are pinned by
  tests, because the failure mode of the opposite choice is a spec that documents responses
  the service never sends.
* **The body shape comes from the call, not from a house style.** `HTTPException` produces
  `{"detail": …}` and `JSONResponse(content={"error": …})` produces `{"error": …}`, and this
  repository uses both — 566 of the former against 17 of the latter, the latter concentrated
  in marketplace. Documenting all of them as `{"detail": …}` because that is FastAPI's
  idiom would have moved V23-76's disagreement from the status code into the body. Where one
  handler answers the same code in two shapes, the spec says `anyOf` rather than choosing.
* **`status.HTTP_404_NOT_FOUND` counts.** 169 call sites spell the code that way rather than
  as an int. A first version of the scan matched only `ast.Constant` and silently found none
  of them, which would have left coordinator-api, blockchain-node and wallet almost bare
  while looking like it worked on marketplace and agent-coordinator.

An existing `responses={…}` on a route is never overwritten. A `{**base, "error": …}` body
documents the code and no schema, because the keys that parse are a subset and a subset
published as the body is a schema the client fails to match.

Dependencies are scanned for one reason and it earns its place: the security and
rate-limiting dependencies raise the **41 `401`s and 19 `429`s** that no handler body
mentions and that a client is most likely to meet.

#### The specs were already stale, and the guard that would have said so had not been run

Regenerating with the enrichment turned *off* — the same code `main` runs — still differs
from what is committed in three of the five specs. `make openapi-check` was red on `main`
before this change:

* **agent-coordinator** is missing `POST /api/v1/agent/coin-requests/register` and its
  `RegisterRequest` schema; **blockchain-node** is missing `GET /rpc/transaction/{tx_hash}`.
  Two operations that exist in the running services and not in their published contracts.
* **coordinator-api** has no missing paths but 272 changed lines, and they are all the same
  change: money fields that used to publish as `"type": "number"` now publish as
  `anyOf: [number, string]` with a decimal pattern. That is the v0.23 float-to-`Decimal`
  work arriving in the schema. Until now the published contract still told clients those
  fields were JSON numbers — so a generated client would parse a decimal string as a float,
  which is precisely what `no-float-money` exists to prevent, reintroduced through the
  documentation rather than the code.

The guard was added, correctly, in the release that consolidated `docs/api/` and
`docs/openapi/`. It was never wired to anything that runs, so it only reports when someone
thinks to ask. Making it run automatically is a separate change and not this one; what this
entry can say is that it is green as of this commit and was not before.

#### Noticed and not fixed

Three marketplace routes under the same prefixes as V23-76's — `offers/{id}/book`,
`offer/{id}/rate`, `offer/{id}/ratings` — never answer 404. The first two re-raise a missing
offer into a 500 and the third returns `avg_rating: 0.0` for a service that does not exist,
which is V23-76's 200-null in a different costume. The spec now reports them exactly as they
behave. Changing how they behave is a behaviour question, and doing it inside a
documentation change would put it where nobody would look for it. Filed separately.

#### Verification

37 tests. The scanner is exercised against handlers written in the test file itself, so every
claim about what it extracts is checked against source visible beside the assertion —
including the two negative cases that define the floor: a computed status code and one raised
inside a called helper are both asserted **absent**. The published specs are then asserted
directly: each of the five documents at least one 404, every response object carries the
`description` OpenAPI requires, every `$ref` resolves, and marketplace's eight 404 operations
match the eight `404` sites in `main.py` one for one — in both directions, so a documented
response with no source behind it fails too.

Generation is deterministic: three consecutive runs produce byte-identical files, which is
what makes the drift guard meaningful rather than noisy. Message order follows source order
rather than `ast.walk`'s breadth-first traversal, for the same reason.

Root **6,421**, exit 0.

### V23-81 — a 500, a 200 that wrote an orphan, and a zero that meant nothing · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

Filed out of V23-80's "noticed and not fixed": three marketplace routes under the same
prefixes as V23-76's never answered 404. Probed against a throwaway database before touching
anything, because **the filed description was wrong about one of the three**:

| route, unknown id | filed as | actually was | now |
|---|---|---|---|
| `POST /offers/{id}/book` | 500 | 500 | **404** `{"error": "Offer not found"}` |
| `POST /offer/{id}/rate` | 500 | **200, and it wrote a row** | **404** `{"error": "Service not found"}` |
| `GET /offer/{id}/ratings` | 200 with zeros | 200 with zeros | **404** `{"error": "Service not found"}` |

`rate` was the one worth probing for. It did not fail — it succeeded, on a service that does
not exist, and returned the created rating. `add_service_rating` never checked, and the
aggregate update it calls afterwards is guarded by `if service:`, so the row was written and
no `avg_rating` anywhere was ever moved by it.

That produces a read that contradicts itself. After one rating on a service id that is not
registered, `GET /offer/{id}/ratings` answered:

```json
{"service_info": {"avg_rating": 0.0, "rating_count": 0},
 "ratings": [{"rating": 5.0, "reviewer_id": "…", "comment": "orphan"}],
 "count": 1}
```

`rating_count: 0` beside one rating, in the same response. And the orphan is not inert:
`GET /ratings/unsynced` returns it, `aitbc market sync-ratings` pushes it to peers, and
`sync_ratings_from_remote` inserts whatever arrives without checking either. A typo'd service
id in a rating replicates across islands as a permanent row nothing will ever count.

#### The fix, and where it lives

Each route checks before it acts, in the shape this file already uses for the same question —
`JSONResponse(status_code=404, content={"error": …})`, matching the eight `404` sites already
in the file — the same eight V23-76 measured the spec against. The checks sit in the handler,
not the service: the service layer is HTTP-agnostic and raising `HTTPException` out of it would
tie it to FastAPI for the benefit of one caller. Its `ValueError`s stay as the backstop for
non-HTTP callers.

`ratings` needed no new lookup at all. The handler already resolved the service both ways
round — `get_software_service`, then `get_service_by_offer_id` — and already had the `None` in
hand. It just answered anyway, with zeros that a client cannot tell from a real service nobody
has rated yet. "No such service" and "no ratings yet" are different answers and a caller needs
different behaviour for each.

**400, not 409, for an offer in the wrong state.** The only 409 in this service is an
optimistic-concurrency `old_value` mismatch on a governance parameter, which is a different
thing. `cancel_offer` answers 400 for the equivalent case over the same resource — that branch
turns out to be unreachable, for reasons below, but it is still what the author intended this
service's answer to be.

**The race is closed, not just narrowed.** A pre-check followed by a service call leaves a
window: another request can book the offer in between, and `svc.book_offer` raises again. So
`book_offer` also catches `ValueError` and answers 400. Every `ValueError` reachable from that
call is the caller's — the service's two status checks, plus `float(duration_hours)` over a
request field — so 400 is right for all of them. That last one is a second 500 fixed in
passing: a booking with `"duration_hours": "half a day"` used to reach the client as an
internal error.

Not `Decimal(price)`, which raises `InvalidOperation` — an `ArithmeticError`, not a
`ValueError` — and still 500s. Deliberate: the same conversion runs a second time in the
handler, on `amount`, *after* the bid is committed, so answering 400 there would deny a
booking that had already happened. Making that path honest means restructuring the handler,
which is not this change.

#### `cancel_offer` is broken, and this is how that surfaced

The obvious way to test "book an offer that is not available" is to cancel it first. It does
not work. `cancel_offer` writes the status `"cancelled"`, which is not a member of
`OfferStatus` — the FSM's removal state is `delisted` — so `OfferFSM.from_string` raises and
**the route returns 500 for every offer that exists**. Its own 400 branch,
`if offer.status == "cancelled"`, is dead for the same reason: no offer can ever hold that
status. `book_offer` has the mirror problem, assigning `offer.status = "booked"` directly and
bypassing the FSM, so any later transition on a booked offer fails one line earlier.

Two status vocabularies, then, and deciding which is real is the fix. Not done here — it is
not one of the three routes, and patching the string at one call site would only move the
mismatch. Filed separately. The test books twice instead of cancelling, and records why.

#### The CLI would have called the fix an outage

`AITBCHTTPClient` calls `raise_for_status` and re-raises everything, a 4xx included, as
`NetworkError`. Both `aitbc market rate` and `aitbc market ratings` caught that and printed
"Marketplace service not reachable … Ensure marketplace-service is running" — which, the
moment these routes started answering 404, would be a wrong diagnosis of a service that
answered. Both now distinguish the two, using the `"404" in str(e)` test already established
in `mining.py`. Before this, `ratings` on an unknown service printed "No ratings found for this
service", which was simply the server's wrong answer passed through.

The test for that went to `tests/cli/`, not to `cli/tests/` where the other CLI command tests
live — because `cli/` has its own `pytest.ini` and is not in the root `testpaths`, so **87
tests there are collected by no run at all**. Written in the obvious place, this test would
have passed by hand and never once in the suite. Filed; the same family as V23-69 and V23-71.

#### Documentation

`docs/api/marketplace-openapi.json` is generated, so the specs were regenerated rather than
edited: marketplace goes from 8 documented 404s to **11** and 4 400s to **5**, and the
repository total from 119 404s to **122** across the same 700 operations. The `expected` set
in `test_the_marketplace_offer_routes_document_their_404` moves with it — it asserts in both
directions, so the three routes could not be added to the spec without the behaviour, or
fixed without the spec.

#### Left alone deliberately

`sync_ratings_from_remote` still accepts a rating for a service it has never heard of. That is
not the same bug: in a multi-island marketplace the service may genuinely exist on the node
the rating came from, and rejecting it would silently drop legitimate cross-island ratings.
The orphan-producing path was the local `rate` route, and that is the one closed.

#### Verification

Marketplace 30, CLI 5, OpenAPI 37, all green. The behaviour tests assert bodies and not just
status codes — the point of V23-76 was that this service disagreed with itself about the shape
of "not found", and a 404 in a new shape would have moved the disagreement rather than ended
it. `test_rate_service_not_found` asserts the row is *not* written, because a 404 that still
wrote would have fixed only the status code. `test_get_ratings_for_a_registered_service` is
its counterpart: a real service with no ratings still answers 200 with an empty list, which is
what the missing service used to be indistinguishable from.

The three module-scoped writes clean up after themselves — this suite shares one database and
`test_get_unsynced_ratings` asserts that list is globally empty, so a rating left behind would
fail it depending on order. Confirmed by three runs under random ordering, not just the
pinned one.

Root **6,432**, exit 0.

### V23-82 — the drift guard nothing invoked, and the reason it could not have failed anyway · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

`make openapi-check` has existed since v0.22 and works. Nothing ran it. It was therefore red
on main for most of v0.23 without anyone hearing about it — agent-coordinator missing a route,
blockchain-node missing a route, and coordinator-api's money fields still published as
`"type": "number"` after the Decimal work, so a client generated from the spec would have
parsed a decimal string as a float. V23-80 fixed those three and filed this: wire the guard
to something that runs.

Three things had to change before it could be wired to anything.

#### It could not fail

The extractor printed `✗ Failed to extract <app>` for an app that would not import, and then
exited **0**. The stale spec stayed on disk untouched, `git diff` compared it against itself,
and the check reported no drift for a service it had never managed to look at. Reproduced by
handing it a `JWT_SECRET` shorter than the apps demand:

```
✗ Failed to extract agent-coordinator
OpenAPI specs saved to /opt/aitbc/docs/api
EXIT CODE: 0
```

Two of the five apps failed that run and the guard was green. It now collects the failures and
exits non-zero, because an app that will not import is itself the finding — either the app is
broken or the placeholder environment the extractor sets no longer satisfies it, and both are
worth stopping for.

#### Asking the question rewrote the answer

`openapi-check` depended on `openapi`: it regenerated **into the working tree** and asked git
what had changed. Serviceable by hand, wrong in a hook — pre-commit stashes unstaged changes
around a hook run, and a hook that writes files can collide with the restore. The check now
generates into a temporary directory and compares only the generated files; `docs/api/` also
holds hand-written markdown and two subdirectories that a recursive diff would have reported as
differences. Which files those are is read back from what the generator produced rather than
listed in the script — a hand-written list would be one more thing that could fall quietly out
of step with the apps, which is the bug being fixed. A test asserts `git status -- docs/api` is
unchanged across a run, so the check can never again be what makes the specs current.

#### The stage that runs is not the one that looks like it runs

The obvious home was pre-push: heavier checks belong there, and the config already ends with a
bandit hook marked `stages: [pre-push]`. That hook has never executed. `.git/hooks/pre-push`
in this clone belongs to **git-lfs** — `pre-commit install` was only ever run for the
pre-commit and commit-msg types. Putting the drift guard there would have reproduced exactly
the bug it exists to fix, in a form even harder to notice, because the config would have looked
right. Filed separately; a test pins the drift hook to `pre-commit` and records why, so moving
it later is a deliberate act rather than a plausible-looking edit.

#### The scoping is measured, and the measurement is a test

The filed description put the cost at ~40s. It is **6s**, steady state over three runs — the
40s figure came from a cold measurement. That changes the answer: 6s on every commit against a
7s pre-commit run is still close to a doubling, so the hook is scoped with `files:`. Over the
last 80 commits on main the pattern fires on **21** of them.

Scoping is where this could quietly go wrong, so the pattern is not a guess. Generating the
specs imports **593** of the repo's own modules:

| | files |
|---|---|
| `apps/{coordinator-api,blockchain-node,agent-coordinator,wallet,marketplace}/src/` | 464 |
| `aitbc/` | 109 |
| `packages/py/` | 18 |
| `scripts/` (the extractor and the enricher) | 2 |

`apps/*/src/` — the obvious pattern, and the one the filed description suggested — misses
**127** of those. `aitbc/models/` is in the miss: the shared models, where the money fields
live whose float-vs-Decimal publication was one of the three drifts this guard failed to
report. A hook scoped that way would have slept through the commit that caused the problem it
is being wired up to catch.

`test_the_files_pattern_covers_everything_the_specs_are_built_from` re-derives the import set
and fails if the pattern stops covering it, so the scoping cannot rot as the apps grow.
Confirmed by narrowing the pattern to `apps/*/src/` on purpose and watching it fail, naming
`aitbc/models/coin_request.py` among others.

#### What the spec said depended on who generated it

Wiring the check to a hook is what found this. The end-to-end test passed on its own and
**failed inside the full suite**, reporting 2,032 lines of drift in
`coordinator-api-openapi.json` — a check that disagrees with itself depending on the process
it runs in is not a check. The variable is `DEBUG`, which `tests/integration/conftest.py` sets
for the session and which the extractor inherited.

coordinator-api gates **38 routes** on `settings.debug`:

| | |
|---|---|
| `/v1/agent/*` — register, heartbeat, status, messages | `agent_messaging.py`: in production the module exports an empty router |
| `/v1/swarm/*` — clusters, join, coordinate, dashboard | `swarm.py`: "Mock routes are gated behind `settings.debug` — never enabled in production" |
| `/v1/dashboard`, `/v1/dashboard/history`, `/v1/miners` | `monitor.py`: the entire router body sits under the gate |

They are in-memory, unauthenticated, and say so about themselves. `settings.debug` also decides
whether `/docs` and `/redoc` exist.

So `make openapi` produced one of two different specs depending on the caller's shell. The
committed one happens to be right, because whoever last ran it did not have `DEBUG` set — but
a developer who does, and it is an ordinary thing to export, would have regenerated a
published contract advertising 38 mock endpoints as the API. Wiring the guard up without
fixing this would have made that developer's every commit fail, or worse, be fixed by
committing that spec.

The extractor now **assigns** `DEBUG=false` rather than inheriting it — `os.environ[...]`, not
`setdefault`, because this one decides what gets published and cannot be left to the caller.
Verified invariant under `DEBUG=true`, `DEBUG=1`, `TEST_MODE=true` and
`AITBC_SKIP_ENV_FILES=1`. Two tests hold it: one generates under a hostile environment and
compares byte for byte, the other asserts the 38 routes are absent from the committed spec.
The second is not redundant — if someone deletes an `if settings.debug:` gate, generation is
still environment-invariant and only the second test objects.

#### The test that could not fail either

That narrowing is also how the test's first draft was caught. It ran the extractor in-process
and diffed `sys.modules` before and after — so once anything else in the session had imported
the apps, the difference was empty and the assertion ran over nothing. Deliberately breaking
the pattern produced `.R...`: a rerun, then a pass. It now generates in a subprocess, where
there are no ambient imports to hide behind, and asserts the collected set is non-trivial
before asserting anything about its contents. Two guards of the same shape in one change,
both of which reported success without having looked.

#### Verification

6 new tests, and each was falsified before being kept: the pattern test against a narrowed
pattern, the extractor test against the pre-fix exit code, the end-to-end test against a
doctored `wallet-openapi.json`, the environment test against `setdefault` in place of the
assignment, and the debug-route test against a spec generated with `DEBUG=true` — which it
catches 38 of 38 with no false positives on the committed spec.
`pre-commit run openapi-drift --files aitbc/models/__init__.py` passes; the same against
`docs/api/README.md` is skipped, which is the scoping working. `make openapi-check` leaves
`git status -- docs/api` unchanged. ruff, ruff-format, mypy and the docs link check clean.
Root **6,438**, exit 0.

No spec content changed: `docs/api/` is byte-identical to what is already committed, which is
the point — the guard was right, nobody was running it.

