### V23-84 — 87 tests, collected by a fallback, run by nobody · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

`cli/tests` was the last suite in the repository that no run collected. It is 87 tests over
seven files, and the root suite went from 6,469 to **6,556** by adding one line to
`testpaths`.

The reason it had been missed through five previous rounds of exactly this — V23-69 added
nine app suites, V23-71 a directory's worth, V23-73 governance — is that `cli/` looked
self-contained. It had its own `pytest.ini`, so it read as a subproject that ran its own
tests.

#### The config that never worked

```ini
[pytest]
testpaths = cli/tests
```

`testpaths` resolves against **rootdir**, and that file *is* what makes `cli/` the rootdir. So
it pointed at `cli/cli/tests`, which does not exist. pytest says so, and then does something
else:

```
PytestConfigWarning: No files were found in testpaths; consider removing or adjusting
your testpaths configuration. Searching recursively from the current directory instead.
```

The 87 tests were found by that fallback. Not by the configuration — by the recovery from it.
Which makes collection a function of where you happened to be standing:

| invoked from | collected |
|---|---|
| `cli/` | 87 |
| `cli/tests/` | 87 |
| `cli/aitbc_cli/` | **0** |

The documented procedure starts with `cd /opt/aitbc/cli/tests`, so it lands in a column that
works. Nothing automated ran them at all: `make test` runs `tests/unit`, and `testpaths` never
named this directory.

#### The file is gone rather than fixed

Every setting in it was one of three things:

- **`python_files`, `python_classes`, `python_functions`** — pytest's own defaults, restated.
- **`markers`** — `slow`, `integration`, `unit`, all three already declared in the root
  `pyproject.toml`, and none of them used anywhere in `cli/tests`.
- **`addopts = -v --tb=short`** — a *weakening*. It replaces the root addopts rather than
  adding to them, so under this file the CLI tests ran without `--strict-markers`,
  `--strict-config`, `--import-mode=importlib`, the 60-second timeout and the four
  `filterwarnings` entries.

That last one is the part worth stating plainly: a second configuration for one suite is a
second set of rules under which the same test can pass. A new marker in `cli/tests` would have
been silently ignored by `cd cli && pytest` and would fail collection in the main run.

Deleting it costs nothing that anyone used. `cd cli && pytest` still collects exactly these 87
— rootdir becomes the repository root, so the real configuration applies, and pytest skips
`testpaths` when the invocation directory is not the rootdir, collecting from where it was
invoked instead.

#### Two assumptions about the machine, never tested

33 of the 87 shell out to the CLI rather than importing it, and they did so through absolute
paths:

```python
PROJECT_ROOT = Path("/opt/aitbc")
CLI_BIN = Path("/usr/local/bin/aitbc")
```

`/usr/local/bin/aitbc` is a symlink to `scripts/aitbc-cli` in this tree, so the tests were
reaching the right code — by way of a symlink that has to have been installed on the host. A
checkout anywhere else, or one without it, fails all 33 with `FileNotFoundError` for a reason
that has nothing to do with the CLI. Both files now derive `PROJECT_ROOT` from `__file__` and
run `scripts/aitbc-cli` directly: same file, one fewer thing that has to be true.

This matters specifically because of what is being changed here. A suite that was excluded
could fail on someone else's machine indefinitely without anyone noticing; a suite in
`testpaths` that fails on a fresh checkout gets pulled back out of `testpaths`, and the
finding comes back.

`test_cli_bin_exists` asserted the symlink's existence — a property of the host, in a suite
that had never reported on it. It now checks the launcher the other tests actually invoke, and
that it is executable.

#### What the 87 are worth

Not nothing, which was the open question. 39 of them drive all 14 `explorer` subcommands
in-process through Click's `CliRunner` with the client mocked, including the `NetworkError`,
empty-result and unexpected-exception paths. 12 more do the same for `exchange` argument
validation and `market` registration, and 3 cover credential loading and that a file-backed
wallet gets a real private key. The remaining 33 are shallow — mostly `--help` and exit codes —
but they are the only tests in the repository that would catch a CLI which fails to start at
all: a bad import in any command module is invisible to every in-process test that does not
import that module.

Two of them cannot fail. `test_network_ping_positional_node` and `test_network_ping_flag_alias`
run the same command and assert `returncode in (0, 1, 2)`, which accepts every outcome the
command can produce. Left as they are and documented as crash checks rather than removed —
they do catch a segfault or a hang, and rewriting them needs a decision about whether the CLI
should be reaching `localhost` in a unit run at all.

#### The documentation described a suite that does not exist

`docs/cli/testing.md` was 259 lines about `comprehensive_tests.py`, `group_tests.py`,
`run_simple_tests.py` and `test_level1_commands.py`. **None of those four files exist.**
Neither does the `/opt/aitbc/cli/venv/` it tells you to activate. It reported "67/67 tests
passing (100%)" against a suite of 87, carried two mutually inconsistent dates in its own
header and footer, and no other document linked to it.

So the one instruction anybody had for running these tests named four scripts that are not
there. Rewritten to describe the seven files that are, what each covers, the difference between
the in-process and subprocess halves, and the three things the suite deliberately does not do.

#### Three more files disagreeing with reality about pytest

`docs/architecture/8_codebase-structure.md` listed a `pytest.ini` at the repository root. There
is not one and there was not one — every pytest setting is in `pyproject.toml`, which the same
listing names two lines above. `cli/FILE_ORGANIZATION_SUMMARY.md` listed the `cli/pytest.ini`
this change deletes, described `cli/tests` as "6 CLI-internal smoke tests" (7 files, 87 tests,
most of them not smoke tests), put `tests/cli` at 119 tests when it is 1,012, and listed two
files at `cli/` — `__init__.py` and `advanced_wallet.py` — that do not exist. Corrected, and
both now say where the pytest configuration actually lives.

The `test-governance` target in the `Makefile` still explained itself as a workaround —
"governance stays out of `testpaths` and runs here instead" — which stopped being true in
V23-73, when the suite joined the main run. Two files in the repository stating opposite facts
about the same setting, in a change that is about that setting. Comment corrected; the target
itself is unchanged and still useful for running that one suite. Worth noting that `make test`
runs `tests/unit` and nothing else, so it is not the thing that covers governance, or the CLI,
or anything else added to `testpaths` — a bare `pytest` is.

#### Verification

Root **6,556**, exit 0 — 6,469 + 87, with no interference in either direction: the full run is
green with `cli/tests` collected alongside everything else, and `cli/tests` is green under the
root configuration it had never run under. `cli/tests/__init__.py` makes it a second top-level
`tests` package, which `--import-mode=importlib` handles — the same reason `tests/` and
`apps/blockchain-node/tests/` coexist. ruff and ruff-format clean.

